# What comes round here is the LOCAL t; the variable only gets it, turn by turn. The laid-out turns leave that store out and make it where they hand over to the loop as written, with what stood ahead
# of the turn that leaves. Ahead of the very first turn that is t's start, which the program never stored: where the counter that is handed over is the counter that entered, no turn has run and memory
# is not touched. A text that stored there always was in the tree for thirteen minutes: the first line said `0 0.25 0.25`, the last `24.5 0.25` (the inner loop leaves in its first turn the second time
# it is entered, and the wrong store overwrites a number that the program did store). No hardening word sees it: under them the turn is not laid out.
# RUN(EVENT): compile StoresLeftOutOfTurnsLaidOut mechanisms=all
# EVENT: statistic StoresLeftOutOfTurnsLaidOut 2
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell globalThis.unseen = (value) => value;
